        {
            "number": 3,
            "revision": "24ee224a47e9eb9e66ddc826bed4c1156f04f519",
            "parents": [
                "20d2e853c25fbc0ae4d566f99a4b0834c9e21676"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/89/90489/3",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Jae Hyun Yoo",
                "email": "jae.yoo@oss.qualcomm.com",
                "username": "jaehyoo"
            },
            "createdOn": 1779520239,
            "author": {
                "name": "Jae Hyun Yoo",
                "email": "jae.yoo@oss.qualcomm.com",
                "username": "jaehyoo"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jeremy Kerr",
                        "email": "jk@ozlabs.org",
                        "username": "jk-ozlabs"
                    },
                    "message": "But how do we ensure that this is seekable?\n\nThis seems pretty fragile; we're relying on a couple of assumptions:\n\n - that the underlying imageFd is seekable in the first place\n - that the `tar` process' `/proc/self/fd/N` aligns with the current process` fd (ie, that close on exec is not set!)\n \nIf you can guarantee the first, then you don't need the `/proc/self/fd/` facility in the first place, but just dup imageFd to tar's own stdin. `/proc/pid/fd/N` does not give you any special seek semantics, you may as well just pass the file descriptor directly.\n\nI'm not too familiar with what imageFd is (or is not) guaranteed to be. If it's a regular file that the caller would have opened (ie, we can assume it is seekable), then I would suggest just setting tar's stdin directly, rather than popen()."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jae Hyun Yoo",
                        "email": "jae.yoo@oss.qualcomm.com",
                        "username": "jaehyoo"
                    },
                    "message": "Good catch \u2014 you're right on both counts. imageFd is guaranteed seekable because bmcweb writes the HTTP upload body to a temp file before calling StartUpdate, so the fd is always backed by a regular file. And your suggestion to dup it directly to tar's stdin is the cleaner fix.\n\nI've fixed the code accordingly in patch set #4. Instead of /proc/self/fd/N via popen(), it now uses fork() + dup2(imageFd, STDIN_FILENO) + execl():\n\n```\n  pid_t pid = fork();\n  if (pid == 0)\n  {\n      dup2(imageFd, STDIN_FILENO);\n      execl(\"/bin/sh\", \"sh\", \"-c\", tarCmd.c_str(), nullptr);\n      _exit(127);\n  }\n```\n\nThis eliminates both concerns at once: no /proc/self/fd/ dependency, and the close-on-exec issue disappears because dup2() is called in the child before execl() \u2014 by POSIX, a fd produced by dup2() never inherits O_CLOEXEC from the source, so STDIN_FILENO always survives the exec. Since tar's stdin is now the seekable temp-file fd directly, BusyBox tar's built-in autodetect works without any magic-byte sniffing on our side with this fix."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 26,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jeremy Kerr",
                        "email": "jk@ozlabs.org",
                        "username": "jk-ozlabs"
                    },
                    "message": "OK, so that answers one of the items above, so now it's just popen() that's getting in the way."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 26,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jae Hyun Yoo",
                        "email": "jae.yoo@oss.qualcomm.com",
                        "username": "jaehyoo"
                    },
                    "message": "I addressed your concern in the patch set #4."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jeremy Kerr",
                        "email": "jk@ozlabs.org",
                        "username": "jk-ozlabs"
                    },
                    "message": "I do think this may lead to a neater approach (and avoids replicating tar's autodetection), but would need some work to properly implement. Details below."
                },
                {
                    "file": "bmc/software_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jeremy Kerr",
                        "email": "jk@ozlabs.org",
                        "username": "jk-ozlabs"
                    },
                    "message": "but you're not using the created (read) pipe? May as well just fork() & exec() (or whatever higher-level process-creation facility might be available)"
                },
                {
                    "file": "bmc/software_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jae Hyun Yoo",
                        "email": "jae.yoo@oss.qualcomm.com",
                        "username": "jaehyoo"
                    },
                    "message": "Agreed \u2014 the read pipe was unused. The patch has been updated to use fork() + dup2(imageFd, STDIN_FILENO) + execl() directly, dropping popen() entirely in patch set #4."
                }
            ],
