                    },
                    "message": "Deferring to actual maintainers here \ud83d\ude0a"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jae Hyun Yoo",
                        "email": "jae.yoo@oss.qualcomm.com",
                        "username": "jaehyoo"
                    },
                    "message": "Thanks!"
                },
                {
                    "file": "bmc/software_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 27,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jeremy Kerr",
                        "email": "jk@ozlabs.org",
                        "username": "jk-ozlabs"
                    },
                    "message": "If there is any chance that the `extractDirPath` string can be controlled by an untrusted source, then we would want to pass our arguments directly to execl (or, more likely, `execlp`), rather than constructing a `sh -c` invocation.\n\nHowever: if extractDirPath is guaranteed to only contain a path with no shell escapes, we're fine. It may be worth commenting as such though."
                },
                {
                    "file": "bmc/software_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 27,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jae Hyun Yoo",
                        "email": "jae.yoo@oss.qualcomm.com",
                        "username": "jaehyoo"
                    },
                    "message": "Good point. Switched to execlp(\"tar\", \"tar\", \"-xf\", \"-\", \"-C\", extractDirPath.c_str(), \"--no-same-owner\", nullptr) in patch set #5 so arguments are passed directly to exec \u2014 no sh -c, no shell interpretation of extractDirPath. Updated the commit message to document the security intent."
                }
            ],
